# Revision history for PAGI-Tools

0.002003 - 2026-09-30

  Most of the 0.002002 API is replaced; there are no compatibility aliases.
  UPGRADING.md has a table of what replaces what and a worked port;
  UPGRADING-REFERENCE.md has the details. Needs PAGI::Server 0.002014.

  [New]
  - PAGI::Routing (route, websocket, sse, mount, router, path_for,
    url_for) and PAGI::Compose (application root with lifespan, error
    handling and a response guard).
  - Response classes: Text, HTML, JSON, Problem, Redirect, Empty, File,
    Stream and NDJSON, built by name with response('Name', ...) -- resolved
    like middleware(), '+' for a Response class from another distribution.
  - PAGI::Pages: negotiated welcome, error and redirect responses.
  - PAGI::ErrorContext (error_context): the error an ErrorHandler handler
    answers. PAGI::Request::BodyError gains client_message.
  - PAGI::Auth (auth, auth_result, unauth_result, www_authenticate,
    requires) and PAGI::Middleware::Authentication.
  - PAGI::State, PAGI::CSRF, PAGI::Transport and PAGI::Routing::URL
    helpers; PAGI::Authority; PAGI::Utils::Headers; PAGI::Utils
    path_from_root, pagi_env and environment predicates.
  - request_uri and raw_path_info on Request, WebSocket and SSE;
    PAGI::Request::BodyError (bad bodies answer 400 or 413).
  - Session's session_state and session_store constructors.
  - PAGI::Test::Client root_path; PAGI::Middleware::BufferedResponse;
    App::File from_app_path; the process-streaming example.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF: session => 1 keeps the token in the session
    and replaces it when the session is regenerated (at login);
    an origin check (Sec-Fetch-Site, then Origin vs Host) on unsafe
    requests, with trusted_origins; httponly.

  [Changed]
  - raw_path is the full requested path at every mount level; AccessLog
    logs, and HTTPSRedirect redirects to, the encoded request URI.
  - Stock error responses from first-party components are plain text, each
    replaceable by one option (refuse, response, http_default, default or
    ErrorHandler's handler); a PAGI::Pages page is the application's choice.
  - One coderef convention: a slot that answers a request (every refuse,
    Maintenance response, ErrorHandler handler, routes, http_default,
    deny/decline) reads a bare coderef as a Request handler; a slot that
    hosts an application (mount, URLMap, Cascade, Builder) reads it as a
    native app.
  - ErrorHandler answers in plain text (reason phrase, or a client error's
    client_message) with Cache-Control: no-store; its handler is a Request
    handler that reads the error through PAGI::ErrorContext
    (error_context($request)), may return any application, and declines by
    returning $error->default. The error is re-raised when the status sent
    is 5xx; a failing handler gets a last-resort 500 and the original error
    is re-raised. Status claims outside 400-599 answer 500.
  - References in events are shared, not copied (PAGI 0.002013): Runtime,
    SecurityHeaders and CORS no longer write into a response's own header
    list, nor Debug into its header pairs, which leaked one request's headers -- an origin, a timing
    -- into later requests when an application reused that list. New
    PAGI::Utils::Middleware::wrap_response_headers hands middleware a
    PAGI::Headers copy to edit. When the response already carries the
    header: Runtime leaves it and warns, SecurityHeaders leaves it, CORS
    replaces it (each sent a second value before). CORS and
    SecurityHeaders now also add their headers when the application sent
    none.
  - PAGI-Tools middleware reject unknown options: a misspelt or renamed
    option (CORS `origin`, CSRF `invalid`) dies at construction.
  - The Router's stock HTTP default is 0.002002's plain-text 404 "Not
    Found"; http_default stays the override.
  - URLMap answers an unmatched request with 0.002002's plain-text 404
    again. Builder's mount now is URLMap, so mount '/' takes every path,
    as in Plack, rather than only /; URLMap's mount('api') now mounts
    /api, as Builder's did.
  - Maintenance answers with a plain-text 503 by default; its response
    option (any application) replaces the body and content_type options,
    which now die.
  - PAGI::Auth's requires refuses with a plain 403 and takes refuse (any
    application) in place of status; its redirect is a
    PAGI::Response::Redirect 303.
  - App::File (and Directory and Static through it) refuses with 0.002002's
    plain text, keeping the 405's Allow and the 416's Content-Range; its
    refuse option replaces all four refusals, with the reason in
    pagi.file_failure -- a branded PAGI::Pages page is one choice.
  - The Router's and Endpoint::HTTP's 405 is plain text with Allow, as in
    0.002002; an endpoint class answers it differently by overriding
    method_not_allowed, and the endpoint still sets Allow.
  - CSRF, TrustedHosts, HTTPSRedirect, ReverseProxy, RateLimit and
    ContentNegotiation refuse with plain text (CSRF a 403, RateLimit a 429,
    ContentNegotiation a 406 listing the supported types, the others a
    400); their refuse option replaces the refusal with any application. RateLimit
    still sets Retry-After and X-RateLimit-* on whatever refuses, keeps
    each instance's buckets apart, counts time below a second, and calls
    itself a proof of concept in its POD. CSRF's refuse => 0 lets the application decide, and
    csrf($request)->valid and ->failure report its check.
  - GZip compresses streaming responses.
  - Cascade advances only on a status in catch; a child that sends
    nothing is an error, not a miss.
  - Host data is validated strictly: Request authority, TrustedHosts and
    HTTPSRedirect answer 400 for malformed or duplicate Host; ReverseProxy
    accepts one trusted X-Forwarded-Host.
  - Accept matching uses the most specific quality (an exact q=0 wins).
  - XSendfile mappings match path components, most specific first.
  - The Session middleware re-sends the cookie on a read once half of
    expire has passed.
  - PAGI::WebSocket and PAGI::SSE send one message at a time.

  [Breaking -- see UPGRADING.md]
  - PAGI::App::Router and PAGI::Endpoint::Router are removed; PAGI::Routing
    is the routing API.
  - The PAGI::Context family is removed: handlers receive PAGI::Request,
    PAGI::WebSocket or PAGI::SSE and return a response value.
  - The mutable PAGI::Response builder and $request->response are replaced
    by complete Response classes (response('JSON', ...), ...); respond
    and is_response are gone.
  - PAGI::App::NotFound and PAGI::App::Redirect are replaced by
    PAGI::Pages; ErrorHandler loses content_type.
  - PAGI::App::Loader, PAGI::Middleware::FormBody and ::JSONBody,
    ::Auth::Basic and ::Auth::Bearer, ::Session::State::Bearer,
    ::WebSocket::RateLimit, PAGI::App::SSE::Pubsub and
    ::WebSocket::Broadcast/::Chat are removed.
  - Session takes no secret, cookie_name or cookie_options: the cookie is
    configured on State::Cookie, which sets no Max-Age by default and
    merges its cookie_options into the defaults.
  - ErrorHandler re-raises server errors to the server after the error
    response (when the status sent is 5xx), and rethrows an exception raised after the response
    started instead of swallowing it.
  - PAGI::WebSocket and PAGI::SSE require pagi.connection (PAGI::Server
    0.002014); their state is a PAGI::State object; deny takes a response.
  - A request body cut short by a disconnect croaks instead of returning
    the bytes that arrived.
  - File, Directory and Static share one request-path contract: hidden
    files forbidden by default (Directory show_hidden becomes allow_hidden),
    configured symlinks served, Directory 404/405 where it said 403/200.
  - Middleware Builder's exact-package prefix is + instead of ^.
  - The test kit is as strict as PAGI::Server: illegal sends fail, a
    missing terminal event is a server_error, an app that never responds
    gets a 500, and each terminal disconnect is delivered once.
  - Lint's strict mode rejects an illegal send instead of forwarding it.
  - App::WebSocket::Echo's on_disconnect receives ($scope, $code, $reason).
  - PAGI::WebSocket and PAGI::SSE on_close callbacks receive a third
    argument, $detail; a callback with a strict two-argument signature
    dies, and its cleanup is skipped (the error is logged).
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's enforce is removed: refuse => 0 replaces
    enforce => 'app', and passing enforce dies.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's secret is removed (tokens are random; passing
    it dies); its cookie is no longer HttpOnly unless httponly => 1;
    cross-origin unsafe requests are refused. PAGI::CSRF's verify fails a
    cross-origin request.
  - PAGI::Middleware::RateLimit's backend is removed (it was never used);
    passing it dies.
  - PAGI::App::Throttle is removed; PAGI::Middleware::RateLimit is the one
    rate limiter.
  - PAGI::App::Proxy is removed: its blocking socket I/O froze the event
    loop for every proxied request.
  - PAGI::App::WrapCGI is rewritten on Future::IO. It no longer blocks the
    event loop, streams the script's output, passes it the request body
    (it never did), enforces its timeout (it never did), stops the script
    when the client goes away, and builds the environment per RFC 3875 --
    never HTTP_PROXY (httpoxy, CVE-2016-5385). Unix only.
  - ContentNegotiation refuses an unmatched request by default; strict and
    default_type are removed (refuse => 0 replaces strict => 0); passing
    either dies.
  - Middleware scope keys move under pagi.*, which PAGI::Spec::Www reserves
    for extensions: pagi.csrf_token, pagi.original_client,
    pagi.original_method, pagi.original_path, pagi.request_id.

  [Fixed]
  - GZip sent every compressed response as 200, whatever its status.
  - ETag, GZip and ContentLength corrupted file/fh bodies, worked from
    partial bodies, emitted a start without a status when the application
    sent none, and transformed range responses.
  - ContentLength wrote its length into the application's own headers
    array, so a reused array carried the first response's length.
  - ConditionalGet and HEAD failed on routes that declare trailers.
  - PAGI::SSE every() cancelled the live receive on almost every tick.
  - Endpoint::SSE keepalive failed against validating servers, and a
    declined Endpoint::SSE still sent sse.start.
  - The Response Writer and Stream skipped on_close cleanup on failure.
  - RequestId added its header to the application's own header list, so a
    reused list accumulated one ID per request.
  - AccessLog let a %0D%0A in the path forge log lines.
  - WrapPSGI set no REQUEST_URI and passed characters, not bytes.
  - PAGI::Lifespan re-ran startup on an out-of-phase message.
  - try_send_* with a dropped Future warned and needed ->retain; SSE
    could issue sse.start twice.
  - PAGI::Test::Client shared one lifespan state across requests, sent no
    raw_path, and lost notifications and errors after a terminal send.

0.002002 - 2026-07-15
  - PAGI::Middleware::Session: non-'http' scopes that carry a headers key
    (WebSocket, SSE upgrades) now get a READ-ONLY session populated the same
    way 'http' does, instead of being skipped; nothing is ever saved for them,
    and an unresolvable id yields an empty hashref rather than a missing key.
    See the new "NON-HTTP SCOPES" POD section.
  - PAGI::App::Router: an unmatched WebSocket route now uses the optional
    websocket.http.response denial extension only when advertised; otherwise
    it closes before acceptance so the server supplies the bare 403 response.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF: new 'enforce' config -- 'header' (default,
    unchanged: unsafe methods need a matching header token or a 403) or 'app'
    (issue-only: mint/stash the token for every method, never auto-reject, and
    let the app validate). 'app' exists because the middleware cannot read
    form params without buffering the body stream.
  - The former Context facade gained csrf_token and csrf_verify (timing-safe
    token checks). Both inherited into PAGI::Nano contexts.
  - REMOVED PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's 'token_param' config: it was dead (the
    form-field lookup was never implemented). Use enforce => 'app' with the
    facade's csrf_verify method for form tokens.
  - PAGI::Utils::SecureCompare: new module for the constant-time string compare
    shared by PAGI::Middleware::CSRF and the former facade.
  - Docs: PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's USAGE section no longer contradicts itself
    (a JS getCookie() against an HttpOnly cookie); it now documents the header
    and form flows separately.
  - t/integration/sse-decline-end-to-end.t now skips unless PAGI::Server is
    >= 0.002005, the release that added the sse.http.response.* decline
    protocol it exercises. Fixes a CPAN Testers FAIL against 0.002001 (older
    servers answer an unmatched SSE route with 500 instead of 404).
  - PAGI::Middleware::Auth::Bearer: _secure_compare now rejects undef arguments
    instead of numifying them, so two undef (or undef vs empty) signatures no
    longer compare equal.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF: new 'secure' config (default 0) to set the Secure
    attribute on the CSRF cookie; enable for HTTPS deployments.
  - PAGI::WebSocket / PAGI::SSE: a per-message callback that dies in the
    iteration helpers (each_message/each_text/each_bytes/each_json, SSE each()
    and every()) now runs on_close cleanup before re-raising, instead of
    bypassing it. WebSocket::run() is unchanged -- it isolates errors and keeps
    serving.
  - The former protocol-specific Context wrappers now fire the underlying
    object's on_close when a terminal disconnect is driven through their run
    method (previously it only recorded the reason, so on_close never fired and
    is_closed/is_connected lied). Both also gained on_close() stubs pointing at
    $c->websocket->on_close / $c->sse->on_close.
  - PAGI::Test::Client: start() now drives the event loop during lifespan
    startup and dies on failure, instead of busy-waiting a 5s deadline without
    advancing the loop (which hung off-loop startup hooks and ignored
    lifespan.startup.failed).

0.002001 - 2026-06-30
  - PAGI::Endpoint / PAGI::Endpoint::Router: route-level event middleware is now
    spec-pure factory form, composed at build time. The vestigial call() and the
    old 4-arg ($scope, $receive, $send, $next) middleware form are removed; the
    endpoint owns the value-flow chain and responds at the framework boundary
    (await $next->() returns the handler's response value). WS/SSE route-level
    middleware now fail loudly (unsupported), and a value-flow non-response error
    names the offending method.
  - PAGI::SSE: close() now sends an explicit sse.close event (with an optional
    server-side reason) and wakes a parked run(); close() is async so on_close
    callbacks run to completion.
  - PAGI::Endpoint::SSE: await keepalive() so the sse.keepalive event is actually
    emitted on a fully-async server.
  - The endpoint and router dispatch paths formerly shared one nominal
    Response predicate so their diagnostics stayed consistent. The current
    application-valued contract supersedes that older release behavior.
  - PAGI::SSE and its former facade gained is_connected, derived from the SSE
    wrapper's own state machine (true while started and not closed), mirroring
    PAGI::WebSocket. The facade overrode the base method, which
    reads pagi.connection (not applicable to sse) and therefore wrongly reported
    a live stream as disconnected. Like the WebSocket counterpart it is "not
    falsely stale" rather than a live network probe.
  - PAGI::WebSocket->deny() no longer records the HTTP denial status as a
    WebSocket close code; close_code is undef after an HTTP denial (a denial
    sends a response, not a close frame). The bare-403 fallback still reports
    1008.
  - PAGI::WebSocket try_send_text/bytes/json no longer fabricate a 1006
    "Connection lost" close (and no longer mark the socket closed) when a send
    errors. A failed send returns false without mutating connection state; a
    real disconnect is still detected via the websocket.disconnect event.
  - Docs: PAGI::SSE scope caching described accurately (cached while
    referenced via a weak ref, not a guaranteed singleton);
    PAGI::Request::Negotiate content-negotiation example reads the Accept
    header through PAGI::Request instead of the (fatal) $scope->{headers}{...};
    try_send_* documented as best-effort with a send-method ladder; a cookbook
    recipe on declining an SSE request (404 / 401 / 204 stop-reconnect);
    examples and Endpoint POD migrated off the removed 4-arg middleware form.
  - REMOVED PAGI::Middleware::WebSocket::Compression. permessage-deflate
    (RFC 7692) is a WebSocket framing-layer feature -- a compressed message is
    signalled by the RSV1 frame bit, which only the server's framing layer can
    set. A middleware at the PAGI event layer can deflate the payload bytes but
    cannot flag the frame, so the deflated bytes went out in a normal RSV1=0
    frame (garbage on the wire). It also wrote a non-spec `extensions` key on
    websocket.accept (the event allows only `subprotocol`/`headers`), which the
    reference server ignores -- and that server explicitly does not implement
    permessage-deflate (RSV bits forced to 0). If WebSocket compression is
    wanted, it must be a server feature, not an application middleware.
  - PAGI::App::Router: an unmatched SSE or WebSocket route now returns a real
    404 instead of crashing the connection. The 404 is emitted with the event
    family that matches the scope -- sse.http.response.* on SSE and
    websocket.http.response.* on WebSocket, plain http.response.* on HTTP --
    since those scopes reject a plain http.response.* before a stream or
    handshake starts. A custom not_found app still takes over for every scope
    type. Requires a server supporting the sse.http.response.* decline events
    (PAGI-Server 0.002005+).

0.002000 - 2026-06-26
  - Split out of the PAGI distribution into its own distribution (git history
    preserved).
  - New PAGI::Tools module anchors the distribution.
  - The application runner ships in PAGI-Server as PAGI::Server::Runner.
  - B<BREAKING>: PAGI::Response is now a value — factories build detached
    responses, body methods return $self, respond($send) sends, to_app makes any
    response mountable; Endpoint HTTP handlers RETURN a response value
    (await $res->json(...) no longer sends).
  - B<BREAKING>: PAGI::Request->configure/->config removed; path_param strict and
    multipart limits are now per-call options.
  - PAGI::Headers: ordered, case-insensitive, multi-value header container
    (rejects undef values; to_hash); Response and Request route headers through it.
  - is_sent now reads response_started off the pagi.connection object, not a scope
    scalar, so it survives middleware's scope copy. Adds PAGI::Test::ConnectionState.
  - PAGI::Response->has_body_source: predicate for whether a body source was
    registered.
  - PAGI::Response: charset follows the body — text/html/send gain
    "; charset=utf-8", json() stays bare application/json.
  - PAGI::Utils::to_app coerces coderefs, app objects, and class names; every
    composition point accepts anything it accepts.
  - builder's enable() accepts configured middleware instances.
  - PAGI::App::Router: coderef route middleware can transform the channel.
  - PAGI::Endpoint::Router route middleware are value-flow (await $next->() returns
    the handler's response); App::Router dispatch returns the matched route's value.
  - The former Context facade gained text/html/json/redirect shorthands,
    assert_http/websocket/sse guards, on_default catch-all, raw_send accessor.
  - Backpressure/lifecycle helpers: on_complete, buffered_amount/high_water_mark/
    low_water_mark, on_high_water/on_drain/is_writable, WebSocket->deny().
  - PAGI::Request->multipart_stream: pull-based streaming multipart parsing with
    app-controlled sinks; mutually exclusive with the buffered body methods.
  - The standalone Redirect and NotFound applications build a PAGI::Response
    value (modules kept for the dynamic case).
  - PAGI::Lifespan coerces its app arg via to_app, and reports shutdown-handler
    failures instead of swallowing them.
  - Spec: URLMap sets root_path; WrapCGI builds SCRIPT_NAME from root_path.
  - Canonical scope-adding middleware route through the modify_scope helper.
  - Documented the PAGI::Response subclassing seam for framework authors.
  - Docs: recipes are now PAGI::Tools::Cookbook; Tutorial/Cookbook examples
    corrected against the current API; chat-showcase example uses PAGI::App::Router
    + PAGI::Lifespan.
  - For changes prior to 0.002000, see the Changes file of the PAGI distribution
    (versions up to 0.001023).
